{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789373169,"nonce":"u3R7sExyVrxXcg1Ce6W+bOcHH/s=","message":"Construct, second round: 140 → 105 (Haxe→Wren) and 79 (Wren→Haxe), Wren→Wren 33 → 27.\n\nWhat the profile said after the shadow word: the collector. Three causes, none of them the collector's own speed.\n\n1. Every ref held a core handle on its object, and every adopted proxy one on its Haxe object. Handles are roots, so each collection marked every object a dead-but-unswept ref pointed at, and the Wren cycle's `mark_held` walked the whole handle table resolving each entry. Now a ref roots nothing: its trace marks its object, and the Wren cycle is two collectors in turn — wren_lift marks and claims, an unmarked object with a shadow (and what it reaches) is flagged `PENDING` and left to the core, the core's mark reaches it through a live ref, and between the core's mark and sweep (`collect_garbage_then`, `is_claimed_start`) the record drops what the mark did not reach. An adopted proxy holds its Haxe object through the trace too (`trace_object`, and the anchor for claimed instances via `WrenHeap::held`), so `adopt` takes no handle and the instance keeps one field.\n2. The Wren cycle never ran in this bench. The bridge allocated Wren objects on Wren's behalf (`direct_construct`, `Kind::Ctor`, `proxy`, string crossings) without polling `should_collect`, which wren_lift does at every allocation of its own. So only the core's TLAB-refill collections happened, and outside a Wren cycle the anchor retains every Wren object: 40 MB \"live\" at the third collection, a 13 ms mark, the trigger quadrupling each time. `proto::made` now runs `finish_alloc` after each such object; cycles are ~1.1 ms with 0.2 MB live, every 8 MB.\n3. The rest is per-object work removed: the drop-hooked allocation (a ref) goes through the bump region (`Block::has_drop` atomic, set through the TLAB, which also gained the block table); `alloc_gen` writes the kind beside the size in one TLAB access; the drop pass walks start to start; a lock release with nothing queued reads one static, not a thread-local; a direct send finds its VM through the site's record (`WrenHeap::entered`, set by `enter_vm`) instead of `VM_HERE`; `keep_shadow` takes the GC lock only from another thread than the VM's; Haxe's constructor call builds `this` as a wrapper on its own frame and wraps the instance after, with no `Vec`, no handle, and the dispatcher's direct send through a site of its own (`typed_send`).\n4. wren_lift 9ed5c88 (issue 96426b5 there): `call_constructor_sync` looked its module up by name (SipHash + memcmp) per call; it reads the per-function cell now. That is the Wren→Wren construct's 33 → 27.\n5. The bench's `Tally.new(t)` now declares `#export = \"new(t: Num)\"` like its other members did; without it `t` crossed as `Dynamic`, a boxed Int per call. bench.hl rebuilt.\n\nEach cell alone, ns, load ~8: construct W→W 27, H→W 105, W→H 79. Unchanged: static H→W 25 / W→H 39, method 23 / 42, getter 13 / 23, setter 17 / 35, closure 54 / 49.\n\nWhat a Haxe→Wren construct's ~105 is now (self time): thread-locals 11% (the core's TLAB per allocation, wren_lift's own allocator, the JIT state, `finish_alloc`'s cell), wren_lift's `call_constructor_sync` 8% plus `dispatch_method`/`call_jit_fn_pub` 3%, the core's allocation 6% plus zeroing, the cycle's per-object work 17% (`flag_pending`'s traversal, the between-phases retain, `drop_shadow` from the ref's drop hook, the sweep's drop pass) — every one of those touches a dead object's memory cold, the `entry`/`call_at_opt`/`direct_construct`/`Args::cross`/`made` glue 15%, Haxe's `hlp_alloc_obj` 2%. The 79 on the other side is the same shape plus the trap.","files":null}]}